{:edb/db-before-t 1438 :edb/db-after-t 1439 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534751 50 #inst "2026-10-12T10:37:19.901Z" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186121487 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121488 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186121493 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121497 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186123254 1078 #edb/ref 17592186123257 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186124208 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 +4y^2 -4=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4\\lambda -1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4  =0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,1 \\right), \\left(0,-1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4(1)-1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4  =0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(-2,0 \\right), \\left(2,0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0, \\pm 1), \\qquad (\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 1)$ | $(\\pm 2, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 1),$ and its minimum value is $1.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186124208 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 +4y^2 -4=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4\\lambda -1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4  =0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,1 \\right), \\left(0,-1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4(1)-1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4  =0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(-2,0 \\right), \\left(2,0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0, \\pm 1), \\qquad (\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 1)$ | $(\\pm 2, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 1),$ and its minimum value is $1.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 false] [17592186124421 1074 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + 2y^2 = 6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda\\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{4\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y) = 6$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{2\\lambda })}^{2}+2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4\\lambda })}^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{8{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2+1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx= \\pm 2 \\qquad y = \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, -1 \\right), \\quad \\left(2, 1 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(-2, -1\\right) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(2, 1 \\right) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, f(x,y) \\,$ | $\\, -3 \\,$ | $\\, 3 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $\\left(2, 1 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $3.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186124421 1074 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + 2y^2 = 6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda\\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{4\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y) = 6$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{2\\lambda })}^{2}+2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4\\lambda })}^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{8{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2+1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx= \\pm 2 \\qquad y = \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, -1 \\right), \\quad \\left(2, 1 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(-2, -1\\right) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(2, 1 \\right) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, f(x,y) \\,$ | $\\, -3 \\,$ | $\\, 3 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $\\left(2, 1 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $3.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 false] [17592186124424 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = 36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n![](images/0b/0bae179a1a44fa0e659681ea8da4f648bc72b944ef6e2730079474dde5f141c3.png)\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\, y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z) = 36$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+4(\\frac{1}{64{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\, y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 4 \\qquad y = \\pm 4 \\qquad z= \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4, -4, -1),  \\qquad (4, 4, 1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, (-4, -4, -1) \\,$ | $\\, (4, 4, 1)  \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, f(x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, -9 \\,$ | $\\, 9 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4, 4, 1),$ and its maximum value is $9.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 false] [17592186124424 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = 36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n![](images/c6/c6423b8d22015c99c9ec599c5417cdfd723d764d57d32f06ff49a28ec27c0c9e.svg)\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\, y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z) = 36$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+4(\\frac{1}{64{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\, y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 4 \\qquad y = \\pm 4 \\qquad z= \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4, -4, -1),  \\qquad (4, 4, 1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, (-4, -4, -1) \\,$ | $\\, (4, 4, 1)  \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, f(x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, -9 \\,$ | $\\, 9 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4, 4, 1),$ and its maximum value is $9.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186124612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435021 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186124645 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435022 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186124738 1050 1200.0 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186124815 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186124815 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x, y) = 2\\sqrt{3}x + 12y$ on the ellipse $x^{2} + 3y^{2} = 15$." 13194139534751 17592186045417 false] [17592186124815 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{3}x+12y\\,$ on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=15.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186124815 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=15.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=15\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2\\sqrt{3},12⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}=2\\lambda x \\\\ 12=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=15$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =15 \\\\ \\frac{3+12}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =15 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\sqrt{3},\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\sqrt{3},-2),\\,(\\sqrt{3},2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\sqrt{3},-2)\\,$ | $\\,(\\sqrt{3},2)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-30\\,$ | $\\,30\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\sqrt{3},-2),$ and its minimum value is $-30.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186124816 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186124816 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x, y) = (x - 1)^{2} + y^{2}$ on the hyperbola $x^{2} - y^{2} - 1 = 0$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x, y)$ exists on the hyperbola.*" 13194139534751 17592186045417 false] [17592186124816 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,$ on the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the hyperbola.*" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186124816 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2(x-1),2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2(x-1)=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=1 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,0),(1,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1+1)=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,0),\\,(1,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(1,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the point $(1,0)$ and its minimum value is $0.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186125109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186125109 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x, y) = 2x + 3y$ on the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 13$." 13194139534751 17592186045417 false] [17592186125109 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+3y\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=13.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186125109 1074 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=13.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=13\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=13$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{13}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =13 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2,-3),\\,(2,3).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $(-2,-3)$ | $(2,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-13$ | $13$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,3),$ and its maximum value is $13.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186125872 1064 "The **method of Lagrange multipliers** states that the extrema of a function $f(x,y)$ subject to constraint $g(x,y)=c$ can be found by solving the system\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curve $g(x,y)=c.$\n\nNote that solutions to this system do not always exist! For example, the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ does not have a minimum (or maximum) on the hyperbola $x^2-y^2=1.$\n\nHowever, we can use the following theorem to guarantee the existence of solutions in some cases:\n\n*If $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint function $g(x,y) = c$ is closed and bounded, then $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.*" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186125872 1064 "The **method of Lagrange multipliers** states that the extrema of a function $f(x,y)$ subject to constraint $g(x,y)=c$ can be found by solving the system\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curve $g(x,y)=c.$\n\nNote that solutions to this system do not always exist! For example, the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ does not have a minimum (or maximum) on the hyperbola $x^2-y^2=1.$\n\nHowever, we can use the following theorem to guarantee the existence of solutions in some cases:\n\n> *If $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint function $g(x,y) = c$ is closed and bounded, then $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.*" 13194139534751 17592186045417 false] [17592186125894 1064 "Let's now consider our previous example, where we wish to find the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2x^2+4y^2$ on the circle $g(x,y)=x^2+y^2=1.$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $2$ at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at points $(0, \\pm 1).$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186125894 1064 "Let's now consider our previous example, where we wish to find the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2x^2+4y^2$ on the circle $g(x,y)=x^2+y^2=1.$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,\\Rightarrow \\,{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,\\Rightarrow \\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,\\Rightarrow \\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,\\Rightarrow \\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $2$ at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at points $(0, \\pm 1).$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 false] [17592186281538 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=21.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=21\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y