45417 true] [17592186435021 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186435021 1071 "Find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=3y+3z$ on the ellipsoid ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=48.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186435021 1072 #uuid "21c855c5-92b9-55cc-b57d-bfcc7ea11d89" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186435021 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=48.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=48\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,3,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3=4\\lambda y \\\\ 3=8\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{4\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{8\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=48$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ {0}^{2}+2(\\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}})+4(\\frac{9}{64{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =48 \\\\ \\frac{9}{8{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =48 \\\\ \\frac{18}{16{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =48 \\\\ \\frac{27}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =48 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{9}{256} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=\\pm 4,\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-4,-2),\\,(0,4,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-4,-2)\\,$ | $\\,(0,4,2)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-18\\,$ | $\\,18\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,4,2),$ and its maximum value is $18.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186435022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435019 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186435022 1069 "q-99037" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186435022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186435022 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=25-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y=0.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186435022 1072 #uuid "24aab5f2-d53f-5124-b5bc-fe883979c5fe" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true] [17592186435022 1074 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=\\lambda (2y-4)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1+\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(1+\\lambda )=2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0,$ the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4y & =0\\,⇒\\,y=0,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0),(0,4).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1,$ from the second equation of the system we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1-1)=2(-1)\\,⇒\\,0=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,4).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $25$ | $9$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,0),$ and its maximum value is $25.$" 13194139534751 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-1502cf5f-a5dc-5e05-9215-eccfb24f8048" 17592186435009 "answer-2c1539e6-873b-5a28-a445-ae8ed6fd0bd6" 17592186435010 "answer-71ec4d29-d08a-51b3-8502-091c7326e596" 17592186435011 "answer-723c1aad-7a95-5932-9d73-d4da0d9899fe" 17592186435012 "answer-74f6294a-88a3-5d3b-88cc-14fd7eb79196" 17592186435013 "answer-7ed5b1f0-05f9-560f-88c6-5e0131619d51" 17592186435014 "answer-9cee8778-b90f-5b02-a141-07a6688cbe28" 17592186435015 "answer-afb0c189-c8eb-5d7d-9361-30bdbdc56df2" 17592186435016 "answer-c8ae345c-fc6f-54f0-b244-c53690489f87" 17592186435017 "answer-e857a965-5a56-5906-8d05-63cb1084b4ba" 17592186435018 "answer-field-73c99f01-9858-5d97-8611-2fe539762d19" 17592186435019 "answer-field-9d6cce28-cf25-55c2-b5f4-befc5a6846de" 17592186435020 "question-21c855c5-92b9-55cc-b57d-bfcc7ea11d89" 17592186435021 "question-24aab5f2-d53f-5124-b5bc-fe883979c5fe" 17592186435022} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
